  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nPenicillins\n\nChief Complaint:\nHand pain\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nHPI: Mr. ___ is a ___ man with history of ___\ndisease, alcohol use disorder complicated by withdrawal seizures\nand recent admission for alcohol withdrawal requiring intubation\nand pneumonia now presenting with hand pain.\n\nPer review of the chart, the patient was recently admitted from\n___ for alcohol withdrawal and Strep pneumo pneumonia. The\npatient was intubated for airway protection due to concern for\ndelirium tremens, and was admitted to the ICU. He was discharged\non ___, and completed a course of levofloxacin on ___. The\npatient reports that he had a peripheral IV in his left hand\nduring his stay that was removed on the day of discharge. He\ndenies experiencing any pain, redness, or swelling of the hand\nwhile the IV was in place.\n\nWhile at rehab on ___, he developed left hand redness, pain, \nand\nswelling. The swelling and pain made is difficult to make a \nfist.\nHe had no new peripheral IVs in the hand. No trauma. No fevers \nor\nchills. He presented to ___ for further evaluation. There, he\nwas afebrile. Labs notable for WBC 8.5, Hb 9.7, plt 601; Na 130.\nImaging notable for left hand cellulitis. Patient given\nvancomycin and cefazolin. Patient was subsequently transferred \nto\n___ for hand surgery evaluation.\n\nIn the ED, vitals: 97.3 65 158/78 18 98% RA \nExam notable for: Per hand consult:\nLEFT upper extremity: Skin intact. No punctum or skin openings.\nNoted dorsal soft tissue edema with associated erythema. \nCentered over the wrist not extending up into forearm. Forearm\nsoft, non-tender. No pain with ___. Active ROM intact. Fires\nAIN, PIN, IO. SILT MRU. 2+ ulnar pulse.\nLabs notable for: WBC 7, Hb 8.7, plt 510, INR 1.3; Na 132; CRP\n144\nConsults: Hand\nPatient given: Ceftriaxone 1 gm, vancomycin 1 gm, Keppra 500 mg,\ncarbidopa-levodopa ___, thiamin 100 mg, labetalol 200 mg\n\nOn arrival to the floor, the patient reports that he has \nmoderate\npain in his hand but otherwise feels well without complaints. He\ndenies any cough or shortness of breath. \n\nROS: Pertinent positives and negatives as noted in the HPI. All\nother systems were reviewed and are negative.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nMr. ___ is a ___ man with history of ___ \ndisease, alcohol use disorder complicated by\nwithdrawal seizures and recent admission for alcohol withdrawal \nrequiring intubation and pneumonia presented with hand pain and \nfound to have cellulitis with abscess s/p incision and drainage \nby hand surgery treated with IV Vancomycin and Ceftriaxone for 3 \ndays and transitioned to Doxycycline for 7 additional days on \ndischarge. Patient will follow-up with hand surgery as \noutpatient for removal of stitches. On discharge, patient's hand \nappeared without erythema/swelling and patient denied tenderness \nto palpation with normal range of motion of hand present. \n\n # Left hand cellulitis with abscess: Patient presenting with \ntwo days of left hand pain, swelling, and redness, found to have \nleft hand cellulitis with abscess in setting of having PIV \npreviously at that site. Seen by hand surgery and s/p incision \nand drainage.\n- No additional OR procedures were done\n- Follow up blood cultures\n- Continued vancomycin/ceftriaxone for 3 days transitioned to \ndoxycycline for 7 additional days to complete 10 day course.\n- Pain control with Tylenol, oxycodone as needed; avoid NSAIDS\ngiven history of gastritis\n- Hand surgery followed patient while hospitalized and will see \npatient in follow-up in 10 days.\n\n# Hyponatremia: Patient appears euvolemic on exam, however, \ngiven\nthat Na improved from 130 at ___ to 132 here with NS infusion\n(in antibiotics given at ___ no additional fluids), trialed \nsmall fluid bolus.\n- 500 cc NS given on admission with normalization of sodium \nlevels.\n\n# Thrombocytosis: Reactive secondary to infection as above.\n- Trend CBC, follow-up CBC and chemistries as outpatient.\n- Treatment of infection as above\n\n# Scapholunate dislocation: Noted on outside imaging.\n- Follow-up with hand surgery as outpatient for further \nevaluation/discussion, possible this is chronic.\n\nCHRONIC/STABLE PROBLEMS:\n# ___ disease:\n- Continue carbidopa-levodopa\n- Discussed with patient to follow-up with outpatient \nneurologist for routine follow-up\n\n# Alcohol use disorder: Patient with recent admission for \nalcohol\nwithdrawal requiring intubation. Last drink more than 3 weeks\nago.\n- Continue thiamine, folate, multivitamin\n- Follow-up with social work/case management for further \nevaluation of outpatient alcohol rehab program \n\n# Seizure disorder:\n- Continue levetiracetem\n\n# Hypertension:\n- Hold labetalol on discharge, restarted amlodipine prior to \ndischarge.\n- Discussed to follow-up with PCP regarding reinitiating of \nlabetolol given patient's HR ___ while hospitalized without \nlabetolol.\n\n# Anxiety:\n- Continue citalopram\n\n# Anemia: Chronic, stable.\n- Trend CBC\n\nTransitional issues: \n-Follow-up with repeat CBC and chemistries as outpatient with \nPCP\n-___ BP/HR with PCP and discuss restarting Labetolol if \nneeded\n-Follow-up with hand surgery in ___ days for removal of stitches\n-Discussion with patient regarding continuation of alcohol \ncessation\n\nGreater than 30 minutes were spent in discharge management for \nthis patient.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nCellulitis\nHand abscess",
